---
// The product's front door: the problem, what the thing is, how you use it,
// and the evidence. One product, three ways in.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, href } from '../data/site';
import { run, threads, decomposition, measurements, fine, human, pct, share } from '../data/measurements';
import GapDiagram from '../components/charts/GapDiagram.astro';
import ThreadStacks from '../components/charts/ThreadStacks.astro';
import TierStack from '../components/charts/TierStack.astro';

const worst = measurements
  .filter((m) => !m.skipped)
  .reduce((a, b) => (b.median > a.median ? b : a));
---

<Base
  title="PACE — preemption-aware time attribution for threads that are slow for two different reasons"
  description={site.description}
  structured={{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
    name: 'PACE',
    applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Linux',
    license: 'https://github.com/devansh0703/PACE',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: site.author },
    url: site.url,
  }}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="section-label">Preemption-aware time attribution · open source</div>
      <h1>Your profiler knows the thread was slow. It does not know whether that was your code or the scheduler.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A thread that spends {pct(share(run.totals.preemptedNs, run.totals.wallNs))} of its wall time
        waiting for a core and a thread that spends the same time waiting on a lock look identical to
        a CPU-time counter, and they need opposite fixes. <strong>PACE</strong> measures which one it
        was, per thread and per sample, in-process, with no elevated privilege.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        It is three things that add up to one: a small C and C++ library you link into your own
        profiler, an open-source analyzer that reads a trace and reports the split, and a service we
        host so you can try it without building anything.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn primary" href={href('service/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-activity"></i>
          Analyse a trace now
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={href('install/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-download-simple"></i>
          Install it yourself
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={site.repo}>
          <i class="ph ph-github-logo"></i>
          Source
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The measurement gap</div>
      <h2>Two causes, one symptom, opposite fixes</h2>
      <p>
        Wall time is what your users feel. CPU time is what your profiler reports. The difference
        between them is real time that a CPU-time counter cannot attribute, and it has two possible
        owners: the scheduler took the core away while the thread was runnable, or the thread was not
        runnable at all.
      </p>
      <GapDiagram />

      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-pause-circle"></i> Preempted — the scheduler's decision</h3>
          <p>
            The thread was runnable and there was no core for it. No amount of work on that thread's
            code helps. The fix is the thread count, the priority, the affinity, or the machine.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-hourglass"></i> Blocked — the code's decision</h3>
          <p>
            The thread was not runnable: asleep, on a lock, or waiting on I/O. This one <em>is</em> a
            code problem, and it is the one a profiler can already reason about.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Measured, not modelled</div>
      <h2>The run that makes the case</h2>
      <p>
        {threads.length} threads on 16 hardware threads, one instrumented. Every thread lost roughly
        {' '}{pct(share(run.totals.preemptedNs, run.totals.wallNs))} of its wall time to preemption
        while never blocking on anything. A conventional profiler reports
        the {human(run.totals.onCpuNs / threads.length)} of CPU time and leaves the rest unattributed.
      </p>
      <ThreadStacks />
      <div class="cta-row" style="margin-top:-8px">
        <a class="btn" href={href('research/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-chart-line-up"></i>
          The full evidence
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Three ways in</div>
      <h2>Link it, run it, or post us a trace</h2>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="flow-row">
          <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-plugs" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
          <div>
            <h3>Link the library into your own profiler</h3>
            <p>
              One call per sample on the thread being profiled, a stable C ABI, no allocation on the
              hot path, no background thread, no IPC and no trace buffer. Your product keeps its own
              model; PACE supplies the attribution it currently has to guess at.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-row">
          <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-terminal-window" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
          <div>
            <h3>Run the analyzer on a trace you already have</h3>
            <p>
              <span class="mono">pace analyze trace.jsonl</span> gives a per-thread attribution, a
              ranked findings list, and a self-contained HTML report. It reads PACE's own traces and
              traces from perf, so you can evaluate the split on evidence you already collected.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-row">
          <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-cloud-arrow-up" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
          <div>
            <h3>Or post the trace to the service we host</h3>
            <p>
              The same analyzer runs as a hosted service. Send it a trace over HTTP, get the report
              back. Nothing is stored, and the service is the same open-source code you can run
              yourself — this exists so you do not have to build a Rust toolchain to see what PACE
              says about your run.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <TierStack />
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Why you can trust the numbers</div>
      <h2>Every figure is checked against a source that is not PACE</h2>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-check-circle"></i> The kernel's own accounting</h3>
          <p>
            On-CPU time agrees with <span class="mono">/proc/&lt;tid&gt;/schedstat</span> — which the
            kernel writes and PACE never touches — to a median of
            {' '}{fine(measurements.find((m) => m.short === 'on-CPU vs schedstat')!.median)}.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-scales"></i> The invariant, on every sample</h3>
          <p>
            <span class="mono">wall == on_cpu + preempted + blocked</span> is checked as the trace is
            read, and it held exactly on all
            {' '}{decomposition.samples.toLocaleString('en-US')} samples of the recorded run, with
            {' '}{decomposition.violating} violations.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">Where the claim stops</div>
        <p>
          Intel VTune already separates preemption wait from inactive sync wait on Linux, and Perfetto
          is already an embeddable SDK. The claim here is not that nobody can do this. It is narrower:
          portable, embeddable, privilege-free and in-process — and it survives contact with both
          those tools. <a href={href('research/prior-art/')}>The comparison is on the research page</a>,
          including the column where each of them wins.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">What it is not</div>
      <h2>PACE is not a profiler</h2>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-stack"></i> No stacks</h3>
          <p>It does not unwind stacks or symbolise frames. Deciding which function burned the time is your profiler's job.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-cpu"></i> No GPU, no cache</h3>
          <p>Different counters, different product. PACE answers one question and answers it exactly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-warning-circle"></i> No invented zeros</h3>
          <p>
            Where a platform cannot make a distinction, PACE reports it as unavailable. A dash is not
            a zero, and the report never confuses them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Start</div>
      <h2>Two minutes, no account</h2>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel terminal-block">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-terminal"></i> From a checkout</h3>
          <pre><code>{`git clone ${site.repo}
cd PACE/pace-analyzer
cargo build --release
./target/release/pace analyze trace.jsonl`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Or <a href={href('service/')}>post the trace to the hosted analyzer</a> and skip the build.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-envelope-simple"></i> Talk to the person who built it</h3>
          <p>
            It is one maintainer, and the fastest way to get a question answered is to ask it.
            <a href={`mailto:${site.contact}`}>{site.contact}</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Bugs and feature requests go to
            {' '}<a href={site.issues}>the issue tracker</a>; the design decisions and the measurement
            method are in <a href={href('research/')}>the research section</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
